+### Per-ray Volume Culling
+- **Definition:** Optimization to avoid visiting every volume at every step. Previously, the march visited every volume at every step just to ask cell_index whether it was inside, and almost always was not. The new method builds an active list up front, so a step only visits volumes whose t-range contains it [S4, unofficial].
+- **Comparison:** Differs from the previous approach which visited every volume at every step. The new approach is pure work elimination and produces byte-identical output [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Standard volumetric rendering is inherently inefficient because it samples the entire ray, even though surface geometry occupies only a fraction of the 3D space. As observed by Piala et al. [PC21] and Hu et al. [HLC∗ 22], only around 10 to 20% of the evaluated samples have non-zero density and actually contribute to the final image [S2]. To address this, acceleration methods aim to localize the surface interface and concentrate samples only in these relevant regions, effectively skipping empty space [S2]. Existing approaches generally fall into four categories based on how they define this region of interest: (1) using sparse data structures [SSC22], (2) leveraging depth oracles [NSP∗ 21,LPX∗ 22], (3) guiding sampling via Signed Distance Fields [SZZ∗ 22,WRB∗ 23] or density estimates [HLC∗ 22], and (4) employing learned sampling networks [PC21,KNL∗ 22] [S2].
+Standard volumetric rendering is inherently inefficient because it samples the entire ray, even though surface geometry occupies only a fraction of the 3D space [S3]. As observed by Piala et al. [PC21] and Hu et al. [HLC∗ 22], only around 10 to 20% of the evaluated samples have non-zero density and actually contribute to the final image [S3]. To address this, acceleration methods aim to localize the surface interface and concentrate samples only in these relevant regions, effectively skipping empty space [S3]. Existing approaches generally fall into four categories based on how they define this region of interest: (1) using sparse data structures [SSC22], (2) leveraging depth oracles [NSP∗ 21,LPX∗ 22], (3) guiding sampling via Signed Distance Fields [SZZ∗ 22,WRB∗ 23] or density estimates [HLC∗ 22], and (4) employing learned sampling networks [PC21,KNL∗ 22] [S3].
 
-For large-scale aerosol visualization (smoke, fog, dust), traditional ray casting algorithms suffer from low efficiency, slow speed, and large resource consumption [S1]. A method proposed in [S1] optimizes the algorithm through a hierarchical bounding box tree to avoid sampling empty spaces and invalid voxels, combining this with an illumination model to enhance color rendering.
+For large-scale aerosol visualization (smoke, fog, dust), traditional ray casting algorithms suffer from low efficiency, slow speed, and large resource consumption [S2]. A method proposed in [S2] optimizes the algorithm through a hierarchical bounding box tree to avoid sampling empty spaces and invalid voxels, combining this with an illumination model to enhance color rendering.
+
+In unstructured volume rendering, traditional rasterization requires sorting 20–40 GB of unstructured elements front-to-back on every camera move [S1, unofficial]. Hardware-accelerated ray tracing is used to bypass this bottleneck by partitioning elements into convex disjoint regions and skipping transparent elements and voids [S1, unofficial].
+
+For multi-volume scenes, per-ray volume culling builds an active list up front so that a step only visits volumes whose t-range contains it, eliminating unnecessary cell_index checks [S4, unofficial]. This pure work elimination produces byte-identical output while reducing CPU raycast times from 70.4 ms to 47.6 ms for 9 volumes at 640x360 resolution [S4, unofficial].
 
